TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43011228
TI  - GLP-1 based therapy of obesity and type 2 diabetes.
AU  - Holst Jens Juul
JO  - Diabetology international
PY  - 2027
DO  - 10.1007/s13340-026-00946-x
AB  - GLP-1 is 30-amino acid peptide that is secreted from the intestine in response to food intake. The release mechanism involves luminal exposure to as well as absorption and metabolism of the major nutrients. It functions mainly as a part of the ileal brake mechanism whereby nutrient entry in distal segments of the small intestine inhibit gastrointestinal secretion and motility as well as appetite and food intake, signalling nutritional abundance. It also functions as a second line incretin hormone. It acts on the brain via sensory vagal afferents, but also interacts with exposed neurons expressing the GLP-1 receptor in the circumventricular organs, in turn activating brain nuclei involved in appetite regulation and reward. High doses of GLP-1 cause nausea and vomiting, but slow dosing titration mitigates this. GLP-1 Receptor Agonists owe their actions to these physiological mechanisms and therefore represent powerful antidiabetic agents (A1c levels below 5.7%) and cause major weight loss (up to 20%), but also appear to reduce the systemic low-grade inflammation of obesity and type 2 diabetes and to exert beneficial action on the blood vessels, providing the GLP-1RAs with preventive effects on cardiovascular, hepatic, renal and central nervous system complications. Orally active GLP-1rAs have also been developed. It is emphasized that GLP-1RA therapy should be accompanied by lifestyle changes for efficacy and to prevent side effects of the weight loss. In addition, any prescription of a GLP-1RA should include a plan to mitigate adverse effects of treatment termination. Nevertheless, GLP-1RAs represent a major breakthrough in the therapy of metabolic disease and are now included among the essential medicines of the WHO.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43011228/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42968811
TI  - Baseline monocyte GIP receptor mRNA expression is associated with reductions in body weight and FIB-4 index after switching to tirzepatide in patients with type 2 diabetes.
AU  - Okuma Hideyuki
JO  - Diabetology international
PY  - 2027
DO  - 10.1007/s13340-026-00940-3
AB  - Tirzepatide improves body weight and glycemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes, but factors associated with inter-individual differences after switching remain unclear. We examined whether baseline monocyte glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide receptor (GIPR) mRNA expression was associated with changes in body weight and the fibrosis-4 (FIB-4) index after switching from glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) to tirzepatide. This single-center retrospective study included 90 patients with type 2 diabetes who had received a GLP-1RA for at least 6 months before switching. Baseline monocyte GIPR mRNA expression was measured by quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction. Associations with percentage body-weight change and FIB-4 index change during the 6 months after tirzepatide initiation were assessed by correlation and multivariable regression. Exploratory ex vivo assays were performed to assess monocyte responses to native GIP. Higher baseline monocyte GIPR mRNA expression was associated with greater reductions in body weight and the FIB-4 index and remained significant in multivariable models. The association with the FIB-4 index remained significant after additional adjustment for percentage body-weight change at 6 months after tirzepatide initiation. GIPR mRNA expression was not associated with body-weight change during the preceding 6 months of GLP-1RA therapy. Native GIP suppressed lipopolysaccharide-induced interleukin-6 and tumor necrosis factor-alpha responses in monocytes. Baseline monocyte GIPR mRNA expression was associated with body-weight and FIB-4 index reductions after switching to tirzepatide. It may represent an exploratory marker of clinical changes, but treatment specificity, causal GIPR-dependent mechanisms, and structural liver fibrosis regression were not established. Summary of the study design and main finding. Baseline GIPR mRNA expression in peripheral blood monocytes was associated with reductions in body weight and the FIB-4 index after switching to tirzepatide in patients with type 2 diabetes. In exploratory ex vivo assays, native GIP suppressed LPS-induced inflammatory responses, supporting monocyte GIPR expression as a candidate exploratory marker requiring further validation. The online version contains supplementary material available at https://doi.org/10.1007/s13340-026-00940-3.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42968811/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42892471
TI  - GLP-1 receptor agonist therapy and incident atrial fibrillation in non-diabetic adults with obesity and chronic kidney disease: a propensity matched cohort study.
AU  - Hassan Abdulraheem
AU  - Okorigba Efeturi
AU  - Grace Chilaka
AU  - Otabor Emmanuel
AU  - Osineye Busayo
AU  - Olayiwola Olalekan
AU  - Oyeleye Tutuola
AU  - Amin Parthiv
JO  - International journal of cardiology
PY  - 2027
DO  - 10.1016/j.ijcard.2026.135000
AB  - Obesity and chronic kidney disease (CKD) are potent risk factors for atrial fibrillation (AF), yet whether GLP-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) prevent incident AF in non-diabetic adults with obesity and CKD is unexamined. We assessed whether GLP-1RA therapy reduces 1-year AF incidence in this cardiorenal phenotype. Using the TriNetX US Collaborative Network (66 healthcare organizations), we conducted a retrospective target trial emulation of non-diabetic adults aged 18 or older with obesity and CKD stages 3-5 diagnosed 2021-2024. Patients receiving semaglutide, liraglutide, or tirzepatide (n = 10,985) were compared with unexposed patients (n = 94,527). After propensity score matching, 7326 patients remained per cohort. The primary outcome was incident AF at 1 year; secondary outcomes included AF subtypes, ventricular tachyarrhythmias, and all-cause hospitalization. Cox models generated hazard ratios (HRs); sensitivity analyses extended follow-up to 2-3 years with landmark analyses. At 1 year, GLP-1RA use was associated with significantly lower incident AF (1.63% vs. 2.62%; HR 0.655, 95% CI 0.508-0.844; p = 0.001), a 34.5% relative risk reduction. Reductions were consistent for paroxysmal (HR 0.586), persistent (HR 0.482), and longstanding persistent AF (HR 0.527; all p < 0.05); permanent AF was non-significant at 1 year. Ventricular tachyarrhythmias (HR 0.556; p = 0.002) and hospitalization (HR 0.668; p < 0.001) were also significantly reduced. Benefits persisted across 2- and 3-year follow-up and remained consistent in landmark analyses. In non-diabetic adults with obesity and CKD, GLP-1RA therapy was associated with significant, durable reductions in incident AF, ventricular arrhythmias, and hospitalization. These findings are consistent with a possible cardiorenal benefit and support prioritizing this phenotype for dedicated randomized trials.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42892471/
