TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43069406
TI  - Cutaneous Adverse Reactions Associated with Modern Antidiabetic Therapies: A Narrative Review.
AU  - Cutuli Fabrizia
AU  - Borgia Francesco
AU  - Vaccaro Mario
AU  - Li Pomi Federica
JO  - Dermatology and therapy
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s13555-026-01982-w
AB  - Modern antidiabetic therapies have transformed the management of type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM), while their expanding use has been accompanied by increasing recognition of cutaneous adverse reactions. This narrative review critically examines the dermatologic safety profiles of sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 (SGLT2) inhibitors, dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors, glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs), and the dual glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP) /GLP-1 receptor agonist tirzepatide, with emphasis on the strength of evidence supporting individual drug-event associations. A targeted search of PubMed/MEDLINE, Scopus, and Google Scholar was conducted through September 2026, encompassing clinical trials, observational and pharmacovigilance studies, systematic reviews, case series, and case reports. DPP-4 inhibitor-associated bullous pemphigoid (BP) represents the most consistently supported drug-disease association, with the strongest signals reported for vildagliptin and linagliptin. For SGLT2 inhibitors, genital mycotic infections are the best-established mucocutaneous adverse event, whereas most inflammatory and immune-mediated reactions remain supported by lower-level evidence. For GLP-1 RAs, injection-site reactions are the most well-characterized cutaneous adverse events, while rare hypersensitivity, inflammatory, and autoimmune eruptions have also been described. The cutaneous safety profile of tirzepatide remains comparatively less well characterized. Alopecia has emerged as a clinically relevant signal with semaglutide and tirzepatide, with recent pooled evidence supporting an association with non-scarring alopecia; however, causality and the contribution of rapid weight loss remain uncertain. Overall, the assessment of suspected drug-related dermatoses should consider the strength of evidence supporting the drug-event association, alternative causes, concomitant medications, and the clinical consequences of treatment withdrawal.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43069406/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43069381
TI  - Lower urinary tract events reported with GLP-1 receptor agonists: a disproportionality analysis of FAERS and Canada Vigilance.
AU  - Selimoğlu Ahmet
AU  - Türk Akif
AU  - Ucdal Mete
JO  - International urology and nephrology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s11255-026-05499-1
AB  - Rapid weight loss with GLP-1 receptor agonists removes lean mass, and whether this weakens the pelvic floor and causes incontinence is under prospective study. We examined lower urinary tract event reporting and whether its patterns fit a pelvic floor or diabetic bladder explanation. Disproportionality analysis of FAERS with replication in Canada Vigilance, using diabetes and obesity drug comparators, adjusted reporting odds ratios (ROR), SGLT2 inhibitors as positive control and a sensitivity analysis without them, and exploratory analyses of indication, brand, dose, onset and co-reported terms. The GLP-1 receptor agonist class was not disproportionately reported with lower urinary tract events in FAERS or Canada Vigilance (adjusted ROR 0.43 and 0.50). With SGLT2 inhibitor comparators, semaglutide was neutral for the composite (0.94, 95%CI 0.85-1.04), with an incontinence signal in women (1.44, 1.14-1.81). Without them, semaglutide and DPP-4 inhibitors showed excess reporting of several urinary outcomes, tirzepatide did not. Semaglutide incontinence was reported less often with obesity than diabetes use (OR 0.66, 0.34-1.28) and with Wegovy than Ozempic (0.64, 0.36-1.16), neither significant after correction, did not rise with dose, and was co-reported with urinary tract infection, other urinary symptoms and, in six reports, muscle weakness. No class-level excess reporting was detected. Semaglutide findings depended on the comparator set, and DPP-4 inhibitors shared its incontinence signal without SGLT2 comparators. The pattern is compatible with diabetes-related urinary morbidity, but exploratory data cannot separate this from pelvic floor weakening, confounding or reporting artefacts.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43069381/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43063617
TI  - Beyond weight loss: GLP-1-based therapies and the reversal of type 2 diabetes pathophysiology.
AU  - Rossatto Thales
AU  - de Andrade Oliveira Ricardo
AU  - Manica Aline
AU  - Cardoso Andréia Machado
JO  - Journal of diabetes and its complications
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.jdiacomp.2026.109453
AB  - Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)-based therapies have transformed the treatment of type 2 diabetes (T2D), producing substantial improvements in glycemic control and, with newer agents, unprecedented reductions in body weight. However, their effects extend beyond these conventional outcomes and include reductions in visceral and hepatic fat, improved insulin sensitivity, attenuation of glucolipotoxicity, and enhancement of β-cell function. These actions overlap with mechanisms implicated in the reversal of T2D following substantial weight loss, raising the possibility that GLP-1-based therapies may modify the underlying pathophysiology of the disease rather than simply control its metabolic manifestations. In this review, we integrate evidence linking ectopic fat accumulation, hepatic insulin resistance, altered lipid trafficking, oxidative and inflammatory stress, and β-cell dysfunction with the development and reversibility of T2D. We then examine how GLP-1 receptor agonists and dual GIP/GLP-1 receptor agonism remodel these metabolic and cellular abnormalities and discuss whether such changes may facilitate β-cell functional recovery. Particular attention is given to the distinction between treatment-induced normoglycemia and medication-free remission. Although current evidence indicates that GLP-1-based therapies can profoundly modify the diabetic metabolic phenotype, durability after treatment withdrawal remains uncertain. We propose that the potential for disease modification may depend on the interaction between the magnitude of metabolic unloading and residual β-cell functional reserve. Prospective withdrawal studies incorporating detailed assessments of β-cell function, insulin sensitivity, and ectopic adiposity are needed to determine whether early and profound incretin-based intervention can translate metabolic normalization into durable, medication-independent remission of T2D.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43063617/
