AU  - Loro-Ferrer Juan Francisco
JO  - Diabetes, obesity & metabolism
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1111/dom.71565
AB  - To characterize body-composition evidence and muscle and safety gaps for testosterone replacement therapy (TRT) added to incretin treatment in men with overweight or obesity and hypogonadism. PROSPERO CRD420261489220. Seven databases and two registries were searched through 4 September 2026; citations were checked on 10 September. Eligible studies compared concurrent TRT with a comparable incretin regimen without TRT or examined baseline androgen status longitudinally. The original eight reports underwent independent assessment. Following joint initial screening, 300 randomly sampled units were independently rescreened. ROBINS-I, RoB 2 and GRADE informed synthesis by design and outcome. Two reports provided non-equivalent body-composition outcomes with very low certainty. Neither established incremental TRT benefit. In a nonrandomized pilot (five men per group), the final DXA lean-mass difference was +2.70 kg (exploratory, unadjusted 95% CI -1.75 to +7.15); the descriptive difference in changes was +2.1 kg, with change-score dispersion unavailable. A randomized conference report (266 men across five arms; comparison-arm sizes unavailable) attributed 26% versus 38% of weight loss to fat-free mass with versus without TRT. These observations do not corroborate a common biological effect. No usable TRT-specific strength, function or safety comparisons or eligible baseline-androgen analyses were identified. Two unclassified registry entries limit completeness. Available evidence does not establish incremental TRT benefit for body composition or muscle outcomes. Missing functional and safety evidence remains a central gap. TRT solely for lean-tissue preservation is unsupported.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43058082/
