JO  - Diabetes, obesity & metabolism
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1111/dom.71538
AB  - Gastrointestinal (GI) adverse effects are common during glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)-based therapy and may negatively affect quality of life (QOL) and treatment persistence. This pilot evaluated the effects of daily consumption of a prebiotic functional soda on GI symptom burden in adults who recently initiated GLP-1-based therapy for weight management. In this open-label, randomized controlled pilot, 80 adults receiving semaglutide or tirzepatide for weight management and experiencing at least one GI symptom were randomized 1:1 to consume a prebiotic functional soda (OLIPOP; 1 can/day, with the option of 2 cans/day) for 4 weeks or continue their usual free-living fluid intake. The primary outcome was change in Gastrointestinal Symptom Rating Scale (GSRS) total score. Secondary outcomes included GSRS subscales, health-related QOL (SF-36), stool characteristics and frequency, appetite and cravings, dietary intake and GI rescue medication use. Sixty-eight participants completed the study (intervention, n = 36; control, n = 32). GSRS total score decreased significantly more with the prebiotic functional soda than control (adjusted least-squares [LS] mean change, -0.931 vs. -0.405; between-group difference, -0.526 [95% CI: -0.713 to -0.339]; p < 0.001). Greater reductions were observed for constipation, abdominal pain and indigestion relative to control. The group difference in overall SF-36 scores was also significant (p = 0.037). Four weeks of daily prebiotic functional soda consumption was associated with greater reductions in participant-reported GSRS scores compared with usual fluid intake among adults receiving GLP-1-based therapy for weight management. These preliminary findings warrant confirmation in larger, blinded trials using a matched beverage control. ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier: NCT07744360.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43058048/
