AB  - Comparative cardiovascular data on glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) agonists versus dual glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP)/GLP-1 agonists in atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) without diabetes are limited. This propensity score-matched retrospective TriNetX study compared adults aged ≥45 years with body mass index ≥27 kg/m2 and ASCVD initiating treatment with semaglutide or tirzepatide. Hazard ratios (HRs) with 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were used to analyze 3-point major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE: all-cause mortality, myocardial infarction, or ischemic stroke) and 5-point MACE (3-point MACE plus heart failure exacerbation and revascularization). After matching, 5665 patients were analyzed per group. No statistically significant differences were observed between semaglutide and tirzepatide in 5-point MACE (19.5% vs 17.1%; HR 1.03; 95% CI 0.94-1.12), 3-point MACE (17.4% vs 15.4%; HR 1.04; 95% CI 0.95-1.14), or any individual component. Semaglutide and tirzepatide were associated with similar cardiovascular outcomes. Prospective head-to-head trials are warranted to validate these results.
