AB  - To characterize serum calcitonin dynamics during six months of tirzepatide therapy for obesity and explore whether early changes are associated with subsequent biochemical behavior. This prospective observational study enrolled 77 adults with obesity initiating tirzepatide. One participant diagnosed with medullary thyroid carcinoma at baseline was excluded, leaving 76 participants for longitudinal analysis. Serum calcitonin was assessed at baseline, 3 months, and 6 months. Longitudinal changes were evaluated using non-parametric tests and mixed-effects models; prognostic analyses were exploratory. Median calcitonin increased modestly from 2.05 pg/mL at baseline to 2.12 pg/mL at 3 months and 2.25 pg/mL at 6 months, with a significant overall time effect (p < 0.001) but no further significant change between 3 and 6 months (p = 0.43). Two participants with baseline calcitonin < 12 pg/mL developed new-onset elevations ≥ 12 pg/mL during early follow-up; no additional cases occurred at 6 months. Early calcitonin change was strongly associated with six-month change (Spearman ρ=0.73, p < 0.001), although prognostic analyses were limited by only two threshold events. Median weight loss was 8.3% and 15.0% at 3 and 6 months, respectively. Tirzepatide was associated with a small early increase in serum calcitonin followed by stabilization through six months. Early calcitonin changes were associated with subsequent biochemical behavior, but the limited follow-up and small number of threshold events preclude conclusions regarding prognostic cutoffs or monitoring strategies.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43009382/
