TI  - Knowledge, Perspectives, and Experiences of Women in Kuwait Regarding GLP-1/GIP-Based Medications for Weight Management: A Cross-Sectional Study.
AU  - Alharbi Maha
AU  - Alshammari Malak
AU  - Alkazemi Afrah
JO  - Clinical therapeutics
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.clinthera.2026.10.005
AB  - GLP-1 receptor agonists and dual GIP/GLP-1 receptor agonists are increasingly used for obesity and type 2 diabetes, alongside growing public and social-media attention. Understanding how women perceive these medications and obtain medication-related information may help healthcare professionals support informed and appropriate use. A cross-sectional online survey was conducted among women aged ≥18 years living in Kuwait from March to June 2026. The questionnaire assessed medication awareness and knowledge, information sources, attitudes, medication-use experiences, and factors influencing treatment decisions. Previous/current users were compared with participants who had never used the medications or were considering future use using appropriate bivariate statistical tests. Multivariable logistic regression was used for selected information-source and perception outcomes, adjusting for age, BMI, and weight-management intention. Statistical significance was set at P < 0.05. Among 445 participants, 132 (29.7%) reported previous/current GLP-1/GIP-based medication use. Awareness was highest for tirzepatide (88.6%) and semaglutide (73.4%), although understanding of medication mechanisms and adverse effects varied. Social media was the most frequently reported information source overall (56.0%); however, previous/current users were substantially more likely than never/considering users to report healthcare professionals as an information source (57.6% vs 10.2%, P < 0.001) and less likely to report social media (38.6% vs 63.3%, P < 0.001). Previous/current users were also less likely to perceive that medication risks outweighed benefits (24.2% vs 59.4%, P < 0.001). These differences persisted after adjustment for age, BMI, and weight-management intention. Despite high awareness of GLP-1/GIP-based medications among women in Kuwait, understanding, information sources, and perceptions varied according to medication-use status. Differences in information sources and safety perceptions highlight an opportunity for healthcare professionals to provide accessible, evidence-based medication information and support informed treatment decisions.
