AB  - Metabolic and bariatric surgery (MBS) and incretin-based pharmacotherapy (GLP-1 receptor agonists) cause substantial weight loss, but whether the accompanying psychological improvement is associated with the degree of weight loss or with treatment-specific mechanisms is unclear. To compare changes in psychiatric symptoms, psychological well-being and quality of life after MBS and incretin-based therapy, and to examine the role of weight loss. In this prospective, non-randomized cohort study, 207 adults with obesity (MBS, n = 97; incretin-based pharmacotherapy, n = 110 [tirzepatide 87, semaglutide 19, liraglutide 4]) completed 16 psychiatric and quality-of-life scales before treatment and at 6 months. Weight loss was 33.2 ± 9.9 kg with MBS and 16.8 ± 11.5 kg with pharmacotherapy. Both groups improved significantly on all 16 scales (r = 0.28-0.84; all p ≤ 0.02). With pharmacotherapy, improvement correlated with weight loss on all scales (all p ≤ 0.04; |r| = 0.20-0.45); after MBS, no association was observed (all p > 0.5). Crude between-group differences favoring MBS (5/16 scales) disappeared after adjustment for weight loss. Only emotion regulation difficulties showed a significant group × time interaction (coefficient 3.3, 95% CI 0.7-5.9). Both groups showed improved psychological well-being and quality of life. Improvement tracked weight loss with pharmacotherapy but not after MBS, possibly reflecting less variable weight loss after surgery. The crude advantage of MBS was no longer evident after adjusting for weight loss, suggesting an association with greater weight loss rather than a modality-specific effect, with emotion regulation as a possible exception.
