AB  - The efficacy and convenience of anti-diabetic and anti-obesity therapeutics have surged in recent years due to the emergence of incretin-based poly-pharmacological approaches as well as advances in the oral delivery of peptide drugs. We sought to combine both strategies in an orally deliverable dual agonist of the GLP-1 and GIP receptors. We designed a panel of unimolecular, fatty-acylated peptide dual agonists, which was screened for receptor potencies in cell-based assay systems. NNC0130 was selected to advance into clinical studies using both oral and subcutaneous administration. NNC0130 exhibited in vitro potency comparable to semaglutide at human GLP-1R and to tirzepatide at human GIPR, with a chemical structure compatible with semi-recombinant production. Its human half-life of 76-89 h was deemed suitable for once-daily oral administration. In a multiple-ascending dose trial in participants with overweight or obesity and with separate subcutaneous and oral cohorts, daily subcutaneous administration of NNC0130 escalating from 0.3 to 3.0 mg per dose provided a placebo-corrected mean body weight loss of 9% after 12 weeks of treatment and 12% after 15 weeks. Daily oral administration escalating from 5 to 50 mg per dose provided a placebo-corrected mean body weight loss of 4% after 12 weeks, which was not statistically significantly different from zero. The relative oral bioavailability when co-formulated with permeation enhancer sodium N-(8-[2-hydroxybenzoyl]amino)caprylate was estimated to 1.6-2.2% versus s. c. administration. The modest weight-lowering efficacy of orally administered NNC0130 highlights the continued challenges of low oral bioavailability of acylated peptides. However, the substantial efficacy observed with subcutaneous administration motivated advancement into Phase 2 clinical development.
