DO  - 10.1080/14779072.2026.2757946
AB  - Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) accounts for over half of all heart failure hospitalizations and is increasing in prevalence, largely driven by obesity and type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM). Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) and the dual GLP-1/GIP agonist, tirzepatide, have been shown to be safe and effective medications in the treatment of T2DM and obesity, as well as in obesity-related HFpEF. This review summarizes the pathophysiology of obesity-related HFpEF, mechanisms by which GLP-1 RAs and tirzepatide may improve symptoms and outcomes, and summarize evidence from recent randomized controlled and observational trials across the disease spectrum in which they have been studied. Therapeutic implications and future research directions are also discussed. A structured literature search was performed using PubMed/MEDLINE and the Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials (CENTRAL) up to August 2025. GLP-1 receptor agonists and tirzepatide represent a promising therapeutic advance for obesity-related HFpEF. Benefits are likely mediated through weight‑loss‑dependent and weight-loss‑independent mechanisms. Important uncertainties remain regarding their role in non-obese HFpEF, optimal patient selection, and their safety in HFrEF.
