ID  - 42957176
TI  - Neuropsychiatric Safety of GLP-1 Receptor Agonists and Tirzepatide in Adults with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus or Obesity.
AU  - Jani Het Tushar
AU  - Levine Helisse
AU  - Patel Abhiraj
AU  - Trivedi Richa
AU  - Patel Xitij Dineshkumar
JO  - Rambam Maimonides medical journal
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.5041/RMMJ.10585
AB  - Neuropsychiatric safety concerns about glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) and tirzepatide prompted regulatory review by the European Medicines Agency (EMA) and the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA). We synthesized the contemporary evidence in adults with type 2 diabetes mellitus or obesity, emphasizing study design, risk of bias, and the distinction between signal detection and causal inference. The PubMed/MEDLINE, Embase, Web of Science, and Scopus databases were searched without a lower date limit through March 15, 2026 (English, adults ≥18 years). The review was reported with reference to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) 2020, and the PRISMA-S and PRISMA-Harms extensions. Evidence was grouped into prespecified categories: randomized syntheses, comparative observational studies and target-trial emulations, outlier non-active-comparator studies, pharmacovigilance analyses, and regulatory reviews. Risk of bias was appraised with the Cochrane Risk of Bias 2 tool and with Risk of Bias in Non-randomised Studies of Interventions; certainty was summarized using Grading of Recommendations Assessment, Development and Evaluation. Twenty studies and two regulatory reviews met the inclusion criteria. Randomized evidence, including two large 2025 meta-analyses and post hoc pooled analyses of semaglutide and tirzepatide, showed no increased risk of suicidality, depression, or anxiety, though psychiatric exclusion criteria limited generalizability. Active-comparator cohorts from the United States, Scandinavia, and the United Kingdom likewise showed no clear increase after confounding control. Passive-comparator and disproportionality analyses generated signals but could not separate drug effects from baseline psychiatric risk, obesity-related distress, or reporting bias. The EMA and FDA conclusions do not support a causal class-wide association between GLP-1 RAs and suicidal ideation or behavior. Evidence does not support a clinically meaningful increase in neuropsychiatric harm with GLP-1 RAs or tirzepatide in the populations studied. Surveillance remains warranted for patients with substantial psychiatric morbidity, for obesity-only populations, and for long-term rare outcomes.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42957176/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42955519
TI  - Comparative adverse-event reporting patterns of semaglutide and tirzepatide: a disproportionality analysis of FAERS.
AU  - Arslan Turgay
JO  - Endocrine
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1007/s12020-026-04840-z
AB  - Semaglutide and tirzepatide are widely used for type 2 diabetes and obesity, but comparative post-marketing safety data remain limited. To compare adverse-event reporting patterns for semaglutide and tirzepatide in the FDA Adverse Event Reporting System (FAERS), focusing on gastrointestinal, renal and ocular events, alopecia, serious outcomes, mortality, and reporting chronology. This retrospective pharmacovigilance study analyzed FAERS reports submitted from January 2022 through June 2026. After deduplication, 215,337 unique reports remained; predefined exposure criteria identified 60,423 semaglutide-only reports, 143,126 tirzepatide-only reports, and 853 reports listing both drugs without additional suspect active ingredients. Disproportionality was assessed using reporting odds ratios (RORs) with 95% confidence intervals. Multivariable logistic regression, false discovery rate adjustment, and reporting-latency analyses were also performed. Semaglutide showed higher reporting odds than tirzepatide for impaired gastric emptying (ROR 6.39), intestinal obstruction/ileus (ROR 4.56), acute kidney injury (ROR 2.51), pancreatitis (ROR 2.18), vomiting (ROR 2.11), and optic ischaemic neuropathy (ROR 15.77). Alopecia was relatively more frequently reported with tirzepatide (ROR 0.74). Semaglutide reports had higher adjusted reporting odds of hospitalization (aOR 2.80) and death (aOR 1.21). In an exploratory analysis restricted to male reports, erectile dysfunction was reported in 92 of 16,673 semaglutide reports and 46 of 27,321 tirzepatide reports (ROR 3.29, 95% CI 2.31-4.69; q < 0.001). Semaglutide and tirzepatide exhibited distinct post-marketing reporting patterns. The erectile-dysfunction finding was exploratory and hypothesis-generating. All findings reflect spontaneous reporting rather than comparative clinical risk or causality and require confirmation in population-based studies.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42955519/
ER  - 

