ID  - 42951303
TI  - Tirzepatide Use Resulted in Clinically Meaningful Weight Reduction in Individuals With Obesity or Overweight: 12-Month Real-World Results.
AU  - Chinthammit Chanadda
AU  - Gibble Theresa Hunter
AU  - Dehghan Melody
AU  - Mojdami Donna
AU  - Huang Ahong
AU  - Schapiro David
AU  - Bays Harold
JO  - Diabetes, obesity & metabolism
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1111/dom.71480
AB  - This study assessed the real-world effectiveness of tirzepatide in commercially insured individuals without diabetes, eligible for obesity medications (OMs), including glucagon like-peptide-1 receptor agonist (GLP-1 RA)-naïve individuals (i.e., no GLP-1 RA prescription during the 12-month pre-index period) and GLP-1 RA-experienced individuals. This retrospective, observational cohort study used Optum's de-identified Market Clarity Data (Optum Market Clarity). Adults with ≥ 1 prescription claim for tirzepatide between November 2023 and March 2024, with continuous medical/pharmacy enrollment for ≥ 12 months pre-index and post-index and available pre- and post-index data were included. Baseline demographics, clinical characteristics, and treatment effectiveness (change from baseline in weight, body mass index, and cardiometabolic risk factors) were assessed among OM-eligible individuals who were persistent on tirzepatide for ≥ 12 months. The GLP-1 RA-naïve and experienced cohorts included 230 individuals (mean age: 49.5 years, 72.2% female) and 123 individuals (mean age: 48.9 years, 81.3% female), respectively, who were persistent on tirzepatide for 12 months with documented pre- and post-index weight measurements. At baseline, more than 85% of individuals in each cohort had ≥ 1 obesity-related complication, most commonly dyslipidemia, hypertension, and prediabetes. At 12 months post-index, the GLP-1 RA-naïve cohort achieved a mean weight reduction of 19.3%, while the GLP-1 RA-experienced cohort achieved a mean weight reduction of 13.1%. After 12 months of persistent tirzepatide use, individuals experienced clinically meaningful weight reduction, comparable to outcomes reported in clinical trials. These real-world findings were observed both in GLP-1 RA-naïve and experienced individuals.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42951303/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42948675
TI  - The Impact of Incretin-Based Therapies on Anthropometric and Metabolic Parameters in Women with Polyendocrine Metabolic Ovarian Syndrome.
AU  - Koceva Andrijana
AU  - Janež Andrej
AU  - Jensterle Mojca
JO  - Diabetes, metabolic syndrome and obesity : targets and therapy
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.2147/DMSO.S620819
AB  - Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), recently renamed polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome (PMOS), affects 170 million women globally and is often accompanied by obesity and various levels of insulin resistance. Incretin-based therapies, including the glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonists liraglutide and semaglutide, and the dual GLP-1/glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP) agonist tirzepatide, offer a biologically plausible mechanism for targeting metabolic dysfunction in PMOS. This review examines clinical trial evidence on the anthropometric and metabolic effects of incretin-based therapies in this population, including a focus on women of reproductive age, who face distinct challenges related to preconception planning, treatment duration, and post-discontinuation metabolic and weight management. Available literature shows that liraglutide, semaglutide and tirzepatide produce clinically meaningful reductions in body weight, body mass index (BMI), and waist circumference, with some studies also confirming preferential effects on visceral and ectopic fat compartments. Glycaemic and insulin resistance outcomes vary across studies, while lipid and blood pressure effects remain incompletely characterized. Although emerging data suggest that higher-potency agents may yield more consistent weight-loss benefits, available PMOS-specific evidence does not establish comparative metabolic superiority among agents. Interpretation of the available evidence is limited by the predominance of short-term trials at sub-obesity doses, frequent co-administration with metformin, and the absence of phenotype-stratified data, leaving the full metabolic potential of these agents in PMOS incompletely defined. Larger, longer, and higher-dose trials are needed to fully characterize the anthropometric and metabolic potential of these agents in PMOS.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42948675/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42948670
TI  - Pancreatic and Gallbladder Adverse Events Associated with Tirzepatide in Obesity Management: A Scoping Review.
AU  - Kutsyba Tetiana
AU  - Raj Sonika
AU  - van der Berg Malisha
JO  - Diabetes, metabolic syndrome and obesity : targets and therapy
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.2147/DMSO.S637243
AB  - Obesity is a chronic and relapsing condition that affects all demographics and results in comorbidities, rising healthcare costs, and social challenges. Tirzepatide, a novel dual glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP)/glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonist, has emerged as a promising therapy offering significant weight reduction. However, uncertainty remains regarding its pancreatic and gallbladder safety profile across different populations and evidence sources. To systematically map existing evidence on the safety of tirzepatide in obesity management, with an emphasis on pancreatic- and gallbladder-related adverse events (AEs) across randomized controlled trials (RCTs), observational studies, case reports, and pharmacovigilance evidence. Following the methodological framework of Arksey and O'Malley (2005), expanded with Joanna Briggs Institute guidance, this scoping review included RCTs, observational studies, and post-marketing safety reports identified between December 2019 and September 2025. The searches were conducted across PubMed, EMBASE, the Cochrane Library, and the FDA Adverse Event Reporting System (FAERS), with additional evidence identified through gray literature, targeted journal searches, and published pharmacovigilance studies based on the EudraVigilance database. ClinicalTrials.gov was subsequently searched as an additional source to identify relevant clinical trials. Studies were eligible if they examined pancreatic or gallbladder AEs in the context of tirzepatide exposure among individuals with obesity. The review included 11 RCTs, 5 observational studies, 4 case reports, 1 EudraVigilance pharmacovigilance analysis, and 575 FAERS reports. Pancreatic and gallbladder AEs were systematically extracted and coded using MedDRA version 28.1, following the predetermined hierarchy. Findings were mapped descriptively across data sources without formal comparative statistical testing. Pancreatic and gallbladder AEs were reported infrequently in RCTs. Pancreatitis was reported in 11 participants across five RCTs, with study-specific frequencies ranging from 0.2% (3 of 1,896) to 3.7% (1 of 27), while cholelithiasis was reported in 73 participants across 11 RCTs, with frequencies ranging from 0.3% (1 of 335 and 1 of 364) to 3.7% (1 of 27). Among observational studies, pancreatitis was reported in three studies, with proportions of 0.6% (63/10,002), 0.9% (1/110), and 2.1% (5,882/280,094). One retrospective cohort study reported an incidence rate of 3.84 events per 1,000 person-years among 9,193 participants, while cholelithiasis was reported at 11.89 events per 1,000 person-years in the same cohort. In the EudraVigilance pharmacovigilance analysis, pancreatitis represented 363 reported AEs (6.3% of all AEs and 19.4% of gastrointestinal AEs). In the FAERS database, pancreatitis accounted for 155/288 pancreatic-related reports [53.8%] and cholelithiasis for 143/249 gallbladder-related reports [57.4%]. Four case reports described six distinct AEs: edematous pancreatitis, acute pancreatitis, pancreatitis necrotizing, acalculous cholecystitis, gallstones, and gallbladder wall thickening, each reported once. Female participants, White participants, adults aged 18-64 years, and participants from the United States (US) predominated among reported demographic characteristics; however, demographic characteristics and comorbidity profiles were inconsistently reported and varied across studies. Pancreatic and gallbladder AEs were reported across clinical trials, observational studies, case reports, and pharmacovigilance databases. RCTs generally reported low frequencies of pancreatobiliary AEs, whereas pharmacovigilance databases captured a broader spectrum of reported events. Variability in study populations, comorbidity profiles, reporting methods, and the absence of exposure denominators limited direct comparison across evidence sources. Vigilant monitoring and standardized AE reporting are needed to further characterize the pancreatobiliary safety profile of tirzepatide.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42948670/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42946972
TI  - GLP-1-Based Therapy in Type 1 Diabetes: Balancing Efficacy, Safety, and Patient Selection.
AU  - Rossatto Thales
AU  - de Andrade Oliveira Ricardo
AU  - Cardoso Andréia Machado
JO  - Diabetes, obesity & metabolism
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1111/dom.71428
AB  - To critically examine the evolving evidence on glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1RAs) and the dual glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP)/GLP-1 receptor agonist tirzepatide as adjuncts to insulin therapy in type 1 diabetes (T1D), with particular emphasis on their benefit-risk balance in individuals with overweight or obesity. This narrative review examines evidence on liraglutide, semaglutide and tirzepatide in T1D, focusing on effects on body weight and insulin requirements, alongside hypoglycaemia, ketosis, diabetic ketoacidosis and gastrointestinal tolerability. Early trials with liraglutide demonstrated reductions in body weight and insulin requirements but raised concerns regarding hypoglycaemia and ketosis. More recently, semaglutide and tirzepatide have renewed interest in incretin-based therapy in T1D, particularly among individuals with obesity. Their greater effects on weight and insulin requirements introduce a clinically important safety challenge: insulin must be reduced sufficiently to prevent hypoglycaemia while maintaining adequate insulinization to suppress lipolysis and ketogenesis. Gastrointestinal adverse events may further destabilize this balance through reduced carbohydrate intake and dehydration. Current evidence supports a phenotype-directed approach centred primarily on adults with T1D and obesity, accompanied by careful patient selection, individualised insulin adjustment, glucose and ketone monitoring, and structured patient education. The clinical question has shifted from whether GLP-1-based therapies are effective in T1D to which patients are most likely to achieve a favourable benefit-risk balance and how these therapies can be used safely.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42946972/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42946946
TI  - Interpreting Diabetic Retinal Outcomes After GLP-1 Receptor Agonist and Tirzepatide Initiation: Clinical Evidence and Its Limits.
AU  - Wu Ben
AU  - He Bin-Sheng
JO  - Diabetes, obesity & metabolism
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1111/dom.71490
AB  - Reports of diabetic retinopathy after potent glucose-lowering treatment create uncertainty for clinicians, particularly when studies measure different outcomes in patients with different baseline retinal disease. This review examines diabetic retinal outcomes after glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist or tirzepatide initiation. This critical narrative review uses targeted, non-systematic study selection. It distinguishes disease onset or progression from detection, treatment use and subsequent visual function. The SUSTAIN-6 retinopathy-complication signal remains clinically relevant; post hoc associations with early glycated haemoglobin reduction do not establish causal mediation. Contemporary cohorts report differing associations across baseline retinal strata, comparators and endpoints. Positive signals should be presented alongside their absolute event proportions and limitations, rather than dismissed because observational adjustment is imperfect. Conversely, non-significant results do not establish equivalence or class-wide safety. Current guidance supports establishing retinal status and coordinating diabetes and eye care during treatment intensification. The implications differ between patients with recent screening and no known retinopathy, established non-proliferative disease, and active sight-threatening disease or recent retinal treatment. No universal safe rate of glycated haemoglobin decline or retinal-protective titration regimen has been established. Future studies should align eligibility and treatment initiation, distinguish incident disease from progression, and use scheduled retinal assessments. A comparison between two specific medicines can inform treatment choice but cannot exclude risks shared by both.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42946946/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42935458
TI  - Oral Health-Related Adverse Events of GLP-1 Receptor Agonists.
AU  - Massarotto F C
AU  - Mahmoud A H
AU  - Watson G E
AU  - Arany S
JO  - JDR clinical and translational research
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1177/23800844261491724
AB  - As the clinical use of glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP-1 RAs) continues to grow for the management of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular and renal protection, noncirrhotic fatty liver disease, and weight management, their potential implications for oral health need to be considered. This study examined oral health-related adverse events (AEs) associated with GLP-1 RAs using postmarketing data from the US Food and Drug Administration Adverse Event Reporting System (FAERS). FAERS reports for available GLP-1 RAs, exenatide, liraglutide, semaglutide, dulaglutide, and tirzepatide, were screened from their initial market introduction through December 2025, identifying 364,274 total AE reports, of which 31,195 (8.6%) involved oral, craniofacial, or dental manifestations. Tirzepatide, despite being one of the most recently approved GLP-1 RAs, accounted for the highest overall AE volume, and semaglutide demonstrated the largest proportion of oral health-related AEs. A disproportionality analysis revealed a significant drug-specific signal for dry mouth of semaglutide, while vomiting, regurgitation, and reflux-related events produced a significant signal across all 5 drugs. Sensory disorders, including dysgeusia and taste disturbances, also demonstrated significant proportional reporting ratios (PRRs) and reporting odds ratios (RORs) for several GLP-1 RAs. Association analysis of AEs showed significant associations between vomiting and taste disturbances, as well as between dry mouth and dysgeusia, particularly with semaglutide and dulaglutide. This analysis highlights emerging safety signals in oral health and underscores the need for heightened clinical awareness and prospective studies to characterize the incidence, timing, and mechanisms of oral AEs associated with GLP-1 RA therapy.Knowledge Transfer Statement:Understanding the oral adverse effects associated with GLP-1 receptor agonists allows clinicians and dental providers to more effectively counsel patients initiating or continuing these medications. By recognizing early symptoms such as dry mouth, dysgeusia, and reflux-related irritation, providers can implement targeted preventive strategies-including hydration guidance, salivary substitutes, dietary modifications, and closer oral health monitoring-to reduce complications and improve patient comfort, adherence, and overall treatment outcomes. This knowledge enhances interdisciplinary management of individuals using GLP-1 RAs.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42935458/
ER  - 

